WEBVTT

00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:04.478
Thermal throttling reduces clocks when hardware approaches temperature or power limits.

00:00:04.478 --> 00:00:07.380
A system may start quickly and slow after several minutes.

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:10.765
Monitor sustained temperature and clock behavior, clean obstructed airflow,

00:00:10.765 --> 00:00:14.040
and verify that desktop intake and exhaust paths are balanced.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:17.640
More aggressive fan curves may improve clocks at the cost of noise.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:20.460
Optimize for a sustainable operating point rather than a short burst.

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:24.850
Laptops require special attention to power mode, charger capability, surface airflow,

00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:27.300
and shared cooling between CPU and GPU.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:31.160
Maximum-performance mode can increase heat enough to reduce long-run efficiency.

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:33.720
Compare balanced and high-power modes on the complete workload.

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:37.105
On desktops, unstable overclocks, undersized power supplies,

00:00:37.105 --> 00:00:40.620
and poor case ventilation can appear as model crashes or device resets.

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:44.890
Memory pressure causes allocation failures, heavy swapping, or abrupt termination.

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:46.675
Reduce context, batch, offload,

00:00:46.675 --> 00:00:50.340
or model size before treating crashes as random software defects.

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:54.727
Close unnecessary GPU applications and control background services during critical runs.

00:00:54.727 --> 00:00:57.540
Manage model caches so storage does not fill unexpectedly,

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:00.740
and retain enough free space for downloads and temporary files.

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:03.420
Apply driver and runtime updates deliberately.

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:05.890
Record known-good versions, preserve configuration,

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:08.740
and test one representative model after a change.

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:12.060
Keep a rollback path when new software changes performance or compatibility.

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:15.315
Operational discipline turns a tuned experiment into a dependable tool:

00:01:15.315 --> 00:01:18.440
predictable startup, controlled resources, observable failure,

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:21.080
and recovery without rebuilding the environment from memory.
